From 336830d660928dd62aac8befed8d0e0c3eed0501 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Angelo De Caro <adc@zurich.ibm.com>
Date: Tue, 27 Oct 2026 16:47:24 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] Fix int_mod for zero-padded multi-limb moduli

int_mod passed m->nlimbs to mpn_sec_div_r even when the most significant
limbs of m are zero (e.g. the dcompress modulus m ~ 2^58 stored with the
nlimbs of a 76-bit q). GMP requires the divisor's top limb to be
non-zero, so the result was undefined: on x86-64 it happened to be
correct, on arm64 it returned values far outside (-m,m), which made
abdlop_verify reject honest proofs (abdlop, lnp-quad tests).

Strip the zero top limbs first, as int_div already does, and zero the
upper limbs of the result.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 src/int.c | 15 ++++++++++++---
 1 file changed, 12 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/int.c b/src/int.c
index 5b45105..000101b 100644
--- a/src/int.c
+++ b/src/int.c
@@ -156,12 +156,21 @@ int_mod (int_t r, const int_t a, const int_t m)
     }
   else
     {
-      limb_t scratch[mpn_sec_div_r_itch (a->nlimbs, m->nlimbs)];
+      unsigned int mnlimbs;
+
+      /* mpn_sec_div_r requires the divisor's top limb to be non-zero */
+      for (mnlimbs = m->nlimbs; m->limbs[mnlimbs - 1] == 0; mnlimbs--)
+        ;
+      ASSERT_ERR (mnlimbs >= 1);
+      ASSERT_ERR (a->nlimbs >= mnlimbs);
+
+      limb_t scratch[mpn_sec_div_r_itch (a->nlimbs, mnlimbs)];
       limb_t tmp[a->nlimbs];
 
       limbs_cpy (tmp, a->limbs, a->nlimbs);
-      mpn_sec_div_r (tmp, a->nlimbs, m->limbs, m->nlimbs, scratch);
-      limbs_cpy (r->limbs, tmp, r->nlimbs);
+      mpn_sec_div_r (tmp, a->nlimbs, m->limbs, mnlimbs, scratch);
+      limbs_cpy (r->limbs, tmp, mnlimbs);
+      limbs_set (r->limbs + mnlimbs, 0, r->nlimbs - mnlimbs);
       r->neg = a->neg;
     }
 }

From cc536f79c73a1cce6c8de5120564b15c809d1caa Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Angelo De Caro <adc@zurich.ibm.com>
Date: Tue, 27 Oct 2026 16:47:24 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] Support building and running on arm64 (and macOS)

The core library and its tests now build natively on non-x86 targets
such as macOS on Apple silicon, using the existing portable code paths:

- config.h: default TARGET to TARGET_AMD64 on x86-64 and to
  TARGET_GENERIC elsewhere.
- lazer-in2.h: include x86 intrinsics headers only on x86; implement the
  add-with-carry / sub-with-borrow helpers with __int128 elsewhere.
- aes256ctr.c: in the generic AES-256-CTR, use the ARMv8 crypto
  extensions (vaeseq/vaesmcq) when available. The bit-serial software
  AES dominated runtime (~50s vs 0.6s for a CBDC issuance and
  presentation round).
- src/hexl.patch (applied when unpacking Intel HEXL): skip cpu_features
  and guard <immintrin.h> on non-x86 so that HEXL builds with its scalar
  fallbacks; compute MSB() with clz instead of log2l, which is not exact
  where long double is 64-bit (e.g. arm64).
- Makefile: arch-dependent flags (no FALCON_AVX2/FMA and -mcpu=native,
  which keeps the AES extension enabled with clang, on non-x86); apply
  hexl.patch and pass -DCMAKE_POLICY_VERSION_MINIMUM=3.5 for recent
  cmake; find libhexl in lib or lib64; use Homebrew's gmp/mpfr on macOS
  and link liblazer.so against its dependencies there; build libhash
  (AVX-512 only) on x86-64 only.
- README, docs: document arm64/macOS support and what remains
  x86-64 only (make all: Labrador, lattice-hash).

On x86-64 the compiler flags and lazer's code paths are unchanged; the
only differences there are the MSB() change in HEXL (same results) and
the cmake policy flag.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
